{
 "about": "Titles people type when they name their job (Sprint 71, BL-12.12), and the corpus roles that honestly fit each. Written BEFORE any alias was added and chosen by meaning from a keyword listing of the corpus, never from what the search answered. 'tune' titles were used to write aliases and to decide any ranking change; 'hold' titles were not: no hold-out query is an alias key (tests/test_role_titles.py), and the hold-out is read once, at the end. 'accept' is exact job_role names that fit and 'accept_like' is a list of case-insensitive patterns over corpus role names, written from the job's meaning (the first version listed exact names only and missed siblings such as a role literally called 'Beautician'; the labels were widened once, by meaning, after the first baseline and the baseline re-recorded before any alias was added; 'waiter' was first labelled a job with no role, from a docstring that predated the current corpus, and corrected from the corpus itself: Food and Beverage Service Assistant, THC/Q0307, is a general pack); 'none_ok' marks a job the corpus has no honest role for, where the right behaviour is to not lead with a wrong or disability-track one ('reject' lists roles that must not lead). Run against the real corpus with `make evaluate-roles`; CI holds only the shape of this file (the corpus is in MongoDB, as for `make evaluate`).",
